9.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The unanimous consensus across all 12 colleagues remains firmly in favor of **D. Sepsis**. The clinical picture of severe hypoxemia, respiratory distress, and bilateral crackles on postoperative day 29 following a Whipple procedure represents Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) driven by delayed intra-abdominal sepsis. Alternative diagnoses such as acute transfusion reactions and perioperative myocyte necrosis are definitively ruled out by the 29-day interval. The strategy remains to uphold this consensus in subsequent rounds.
}

